dist/

# >>> morf (memory data; .morf/scripts,hooks,docs stay committed) >>>
# A worktree needs .morf/scripts to fire its hooks, so code is committed and
# only data is ignored. Re-run with --private to commit the whole .morf.
.morf/Observations/
.morf/Transcripts/
.morf/Facts/
.morf/Rules/
.morf/.state/
.morf/sessions.md
.morf/audit.md
.morf/levels.md
.morf/dropped.md
.morf/INDEX.md
.morf/TAGS.md
.morf/TRACE.md
# <<< morf <<<

# >>> morf self-hosting >>>
# In MORF's own repository the install output is a byte-identical copy of the
# source: .morf/scripts of scripts/, .morf/docs of docs/, .claude/commands/morf
# of commands/, .claude/skills/morf of SKILL.md. Committing both would put two
# copies of one thing in one repo, and they would drift — the very defect this
# layout removes. The source is what is tracked; `tools/install.sh` unpacks the
# rest, and the memory under .morf stays local. Consumer repos commit their
# .morf/scripts instead: there it is not a duplicate, and a worktree needs it
# present for its hooks to fire.
.morf/
.claude/settings.json
.claude/commands/morf/
.claude/skills/morf/
# <<< morf self-hosting <<<
